{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"01","section":"05","chapter_title":"Digital Citizenship and AI Readiness","title":"Privacy and Account Safety","description":"CAT 100 textbook section on privacy and account safety, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Create a unique, strong passphrase and stop reusing one password across sites.","Turn on two-factor authentication and explain why a stolen password alone then fails.","Spot the standard signals of a phishing message and respond without clicking.","Strip personal and others' data out of any text before pasting it into an AI tool."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness","cat100_supplement_privacy_and_account_safety","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":5,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/01/04"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_1_5_privacy_and_account_safety_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_1_5_privacy_and_account_safety_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_1_5_privacy_and_account_safety_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_1_5_privacy_and_account_safety_calibration"]},"content":"# Privacy and Account Safety\n\n![Privacy and Account Safety textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/01-05-privacy-and-account-safety.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nYour school email is the master key to your digital life. It can reset your bank login, your social media, your financial aid portal, and your course accounts. If someone takes it over, they do not just read your mail; they walk through every door it can unlock. This section is about the small set of habits that make that takeover hard: unique passwords, a second factor, and the ability to recognize a phishing message before you click.\n\nThe work product is a privacy and account-safety checklist where each item is a concrete action a reviewer could confirm, not a vague claim that you \"made things safer.\" Security is not a feeling; it is a set of settings that are either on or off and a set of behaviors you either practice or do not.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Create a unique, strong passphrase and stop reusing one password across sites.\n- Turn on two-factor authentication and explain why a stolen password alone then fails.\n- Spot the standard signals of a phishing message and respond without clicking.\n- Strip personal and others' data out of any text before pasting it into an AI tool.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nSam reuses one password across his shopping account, his streaming service, and his school email. A shopping site he used two years ago suffers a data breach, and his email and password end up on a list that attackers buy. Within days, someone logs into his school email from another country, resets his financial aid password, and changes his contact email so he cannot get back in.\n\nSam never clicked anything suspicious. The breach happened somewhere else entirely. Password reuse is what turned one company's mistake into his crisis, and a second factor on his email would have stopped the login cold.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAccount safety rests on a simple idea: do not let one failure become total failure. Three layers carry most of the protection a student needs.\n\n| Layer | What it does | The failure it blocks |\n|-------|--------------|------------------------|\n| Unique passwords | A breach at one site cannot unlock others | Credential stuffing from leaked lists |\n| Two-factor authentication | A second proof is required to log in | A stolen password used alone |\n| Phishing awareness | You verify before you act | Handing your credentials to an attacker |\n\nWhy unique passwords matter is concrete: attackers take email-and-password pairs leaked from one breach and try them on hundreds of other sites automatically. Reuse means one leak unlocks everything. Why two-factor authentication matters is that it breaks the value of a stolen password: even with your password, an attacker also needs the code on your phone, which they do not have. Why phishing awareness matters is that the strongest password and second factor do not help if you type them into a fake login page yourself. The three layers cover the three main ways accounts fall: a leak elsewhere, a guessed or stolen password, and a tricked user.\n\nOne refinement: not all second factors are equal. SMS codes are the weakest (they can be intercepted through SIM-swap or relay attacks); app-based authenticator codes are better, but a code you type into a fake login page is captured just like the password; passkeys and hardware security keys are phishing-resistant, because they are cryptographically bound to the real site's domain and simply cannot be entered on a lookalike page. In the phishing scenario below, a passkey would have blocked the login even after the password, and even an app code, had been phished.\n\n**Recent evidence.** These layers are measured, not just asserted. A large-scale study by Google with New York University and UC San Diego found that adding an on-device second-factor prompt blocked 100% of automated bot attacks, 99% of bulk phishing attacks, and 90% of targeted attacks (an SMS code blocked 100%, 96%, and 76%, respectively). The scale of the credential-stuffing threat is also documented: the Verizon Data Breach Investigations Report finds stolen credentials remain the single largest breach vector (about 31% of breaches in the 2025 report), and roughly one in five breaches begins with credential abuse.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Sam uses one very strong password (lots of symbols) on every site, and one of those sites suffers a data breach. What happens to his other accounts?\" explanation=\"Strength does not defend against reuse. Once the shared password leaks, attackers try it everywhere automatically, so uniqueness matters more than complexity.\" options='[{\"text\":\"They are safe because the password is strong\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"They are all exposed, because the same leaked password unlocks every account\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Only the breached site is affected\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Nothing happens unless he clicks a phishing link\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nThe weak version below sounds responsible, but a reviewer cannot confirm it:\n\n> I made my account safer.\n\nThat sentence is too thin because \"safer\" names no setting and no action. Nobody can check it. A strong checklist names each item and the specific action you took, so a reviewer can verify it the same way you would verify a resume claim.\n\n### Worked Example: hardening one account, step by step\n\nWalk through securing your school email, checking each item against the privacy checklist.\n\n1. **Unique password.** Old setup: same password as your shopping site. Action: create a unique 16-character passphrase, for example `purple-train-coffee-42`, used nowhere else.\n2. **Turn on two-factor authentication.** Enable app-based codes so a leaked password alone cannot grant access.\n3. **Test a phishing signal.** A message says: \"Verify in 1 hour or lose access: bit.ly/uni-verify.\" Decision: do not click; open the portal at its known address and confirm there. Mark the message as phishing.\n4. **Check AI data boundary.** Before asking AI to reword an email, remove your student ID and a classmate's phone number, then paste only the text to revise. Which tool you paste into also matters: as of 2026, consumer chatbots may retain conversation text and use it to improve models unless you opt out (in ChatGPT, under Settings > Data Controls), while education and enterprise tiers (such as ChatGPT Edu and the education tenants from Google and Microsoft) do not train on your input by default and can carry FERPA-aligned contractual terms. Check the specific tool's data-use setting before pasting, and prefer the school-provided managed tool for anything with real names or IDs.\n\nChecklist result: 4 of 4 items addressed, each with a concrete action a reviewer can confirm, not just \"I made my account safer.\"\n\nA message lands in your inbox. Walk the decision tree before you touch anything.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Responding to a verify-or-lose-access message\",\"start\":\"p1\",\"nodes\":{\"p1\":{\"prompt\":\"You receive: \\\"Verify in 1 hour or lose access: bit.ly/uni-verify.\\\" What is your first move?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Click the link quickly to avoid losing access\",\"next\":\"caught\",\"feedback\":\"The urgency and shortened link are classic phishing tells. Clicking hands your credentials to whoever built the fake page.\"},{\"label\":\"Do not click; open the portal at its known address yourself\",\"next\":\"p2\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. You verify through a channel you already trust instead of the link in the message.\"}]},\"caught\":{\"prompt\":\"The link opens a login page that looks exactly like the real portal and asks for your password. What now?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Type your password; it looks legitimate\",\"next\":\"breach\",\"feedback\":\"Modern phishing copies real logos and wording. Looking real is not the same as being real.\"},{\"label\":\"Stop, close it, and go to the portal directly instead\",\"next\":\"p2\",\"feedback\":\"Good recovery. The reliable tell is the request itself, not the visual quality of the page.\"}]},\"breach\":{\"prompt\":\"Your password is now in an attacker hand. What single setting would still have blocked the login?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A longer password\",\"next\":\"p2\",\"feedback\":\"Length alone would not help once the password is stolen. Two-factor authentication is what blocks a login made with a stolen password.\"},{\"label\":\"Two-factor authentication on the account\",\"next\":\"p2\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Even with your password, the attacker lacks the second-factor code on your phone. Note that a code typed into the fake page would have been captured too; a passkey is stronger still, because it is bound to the real domain and cannot be entered on a lookalike page at all.\"}]},\"p2\":{\"prompt\":\"You safely reach the real portal and there is no such verification request. You mark the email as phishing. What checklist habit did this confirm?\",\"feedback\":\"Verify before you act through a known-good channel, and rely on a second factor so one stolen credential is not total failure.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\nNow put the hardening steps in the order that makes the safe behavior the easy behavior.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Replace any reused password with a unique passphrase\",\"Turn on app-based two-factor authentication\",\"Treat urgent verify-or-lose messages as phishing and verify directly\",\"Strip personal and others data before pasting text into AI\",\"Record each item as an action a reviewer could confirm\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore you act on any message that asks for credentials, money, or urgent clicks, slow down and verify through a channel you already trust. Urgency, a threat of losing access, a slightly-off sender address, and a shortened link are the classic phishing tells; any one of them means you go to the site yourself instead of clicking the link. For your accounts, the rule is: every important account gets a unique password and, wherever offered, a second factor.\n\n1. **Reuse check:** Is this password used on any other site? If yes, change it.\n2. **Second-factor check:** Is two-factor authentication on for this account?\n3. **Verify-before-click check:** Did you confirm the request through a known-good channel rather than the link in the message?\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nA common mistake is thinking a \"strong\" password (lots of symbols) on every site is enough. Strength does not help against reuse: if you use the same strong password everywhere and one site leaks it, every account falls. Uniqueness matters more than complexity, which is why a long passphrase you can remember, different per site, beats one clever password shared across all of them.\n\nA second misreading is assuming phishing always looks obviously fake. Modern phishing copies real logos, uses urgent but plausible wording, and may even spoof a real name. The reliable tell is not bad spelling; it is the request itself. A legitimate institution does not threaten to delete your account in one hour over an unsolicited link.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nPicture a password manager, which is the practical way to follow the unique-password rule without memorizing dozens of strings. You install it, let it generate a different long random password for each site, and protect the whole vault with one strong master passphrase plus two-factor authentication. Now reuse is solved structurally: the manager remembers a unique password per site so you do not have to. The one password you must memorize is the master one, and it is the one you most protect.\n\nThe walkthrough's point is that good security is mostly about removing the human bottleneck. People reuse passwords because remembering unique ones is hard; the manager removes the hardship, so the safe behavior becomes the easy behavior. Pair that with two-factor authentication on your email and a habit of verifying suspicious messages, and you have covered the three layers without relying on willpower at every login.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nWhen you read a security claim (your own or a friend's), check whether it names a verifiable setting or just a feeling. \"I'm careful online\" is a feeling; \"2FA is on for my email and my password manager generates unique passwords\" is a set of checkable facts. The difference is the same one you practiced with resume bullets: a result a third party could confirm versus a vague impression.\n\nA strong reader of an account-safety claim can answer four questions:\n\n1. Is each important account protected by a unique password?\n2. Is two-factor authentication on where it is offered?\n3. Does the person verify before clicking on urgent requests?\n4. Is private data kept out of tools that might store it?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour checklist is strong when each row names a setting that is demonstrably on or an action you actually took, so a reviewer could confirm it. The earlier layers are the unique password, the second factor, and the phishing response; the last layer is presentation. If you are unsure whether a setting counts, mark it in your trace and ask BigAL to help you find the exact toggle rather than asking it for general security tips.\n\nA useful note would read: \"I changed my school email password from one reused across three sites to a unique passphrase, turned on app-based 2FA, and treated a 'verify in one hour' message as phishing by going to the portal directly instead of clicking. I also stripped a classmate's phone number from an email before pasting it into an AI tool.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the safety habit from your own accounts to a shared device or a group account, such as a club's social media login. The reuse and second-factor rules still hold, but a new constraint appears: multiple people need access without sharing one personal password. The transfer move is to use the platform's role-based access or a shared password manager vault, and to turn on two-factor authentication tied to a method the whole leadership team can reach, not one person's phone. The underlying principle, do not let one failure become total failure, does not change; what changes is that you now design for several trusted users rather than one. Understanding the principle, not just the personal recipe, is what lets you keep a shared account safe.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the privacy and account safety checklist packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/01-05-privacy-and-account-safety-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"privacy and account safety checklist\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"01.05\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show an account in its unsafe state (reused password, no second factor). The revised version should show each item fixed with a concrete action. The trace should explain why each action blocks a specific failure.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a reused password setup with a unique-password-plus-2FA setup.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the three-layer model to a new account or device.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes one of your settings.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising an account-safety checklist for CAT 100 01.05. A peer comment says one item just says 'made it safer' with no verifiable action. Choose the next revision move and the concrete setting or action that a reviewer could confirm.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_privacy_and_account_safety\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\", \"cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_privacy_and_account_safety\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that your checklist records a unique password, two-factor authentication, a phishing response, and an AI data boundary, each as an action a reviewer could confirm.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_5_privacy_and_account_safety_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_privacy_and_account_safety","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_privacy_and_account_safety","cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness"],"stem":"You use the same strong password for your school portal, email, and a shopping site. The shopping site reports a data breach. What is the real risk?","options":["No risk, because the password was strong.","Attackers can try that leaked password on your email and school portal too, so reused passwords expose every linked account.","Only the shopping account is ever at risk in a breach.","The password becomes safe again after you log in once."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A breach on one site exposes every account that reuses the password. Strength does not help once the password is leaked; uniqueness does.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_5_privacy_and_account_safety_reused_password_safe","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_5_privacy_and_account_safety_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_privacy_and_account_safety","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_privacy_and_account_safety"],"stem":"The school portal offers two-factor authentication but it adds a code step at login. Why is turning it on worth the extra step?","options":["It makes your password longer automatically.","Even if your password leaks, an attacker still cannot log in without the second factor, which blocks most takeovers.","It removes the need to remember a password.","It only matters for staff accounts, not students."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Two-factor authentication blocks essentially all automated takeover attempts and the large majority of targeted ones, because the attacker would also need the second factor, not just the password.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_5_privacy_and_account_safety_2fa_unnecessary","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_5_privacy_and_account_safety_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_privacy_and_account_safety","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_privacy_and_account_safety","ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"You want an AI tool to help reword a complaint email. The draft contains your student ID and a classmate's full name and phone number. What should you do before pasting it in?","options":["Paste the whole email; AI tools keep everything private.","Remove your student ID and the classmate's name and number first, then paste only the text you need reworded.","Paste it but ask the AI to keep it secret.","Add even more detail so the AI understands the situation."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Redact identifiers and other people's private data before using an AI tool. Share only what is needed for the task. Retention also depends on the tool: consumer chatbots may keep and train on pasted text unless you opt out, while school-provided education or enterprise tiers typically do not train on input by default, so prefer the managed tool for anything sensitive.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_5_privacy_and_account_safety_overshare_with_ai","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_5_privacy_and_account_safety_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_privacy_and_account_safety","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_privacy_and_account_safety"],"stem":"You get a text: \"URGENT: your student account will be locked in 1 hour. Verify now: bit.ly/uni-verify.\" What is the safest response?","options":["Click the link quickly so the account is not locked.","Do not click; open the school portal directly through its known address and check your account there.","Reply with your password so they can confirm it is you.","Forward the link to classmates so they verify too."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Urgency and shortened links are phishing signals. Never click; reach the real site directly and verify there.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_5_privacy_and_account_safety_urgent_email_trusted","difficulty":"medium"}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_5_privacy_and_account_safety_reused_password_safe","pattern":"password_reuse_is_safe","description":"Believing one strong password reused across accounts is safe","trigger":"learner uses the same password for email, school portal, and social media","feedback":"If one site is breached, every account sharing that password is exposed. Use a unique password per account, ideally with a password manager.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_5_privacy_and_account_safety_2fa_unnecessary","pattern":"two_factor_seen_as_optional_hassle","description":"Skipping two-factor authentication because it adds an extra step","trigger":"learner declines 2FA to log in faster","feedback":"Two-factor authentication blocks essentially all automated takeover attempts and the large majority of targeted ones, even when a password leaks. Turn it on for email and the school portal first.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_5_privacy_and_account_safety_urgent_email_trusted","pattern":"urgency_signals_legitimacy","description":"Trusting an email or text because it sounds urgent and official","trigger":"learner clicks a link in a message demanding immediate action","feedback":"Urgency is a phishing tactic. Do not click the link; go to the official site directly and verify the sender address.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_5_privacy_and_account_safety_overshare_with_ai","pattern":"pasting_sensitive_data_into_ai","description":"Pasting personal or others' private data into an AI tool without checking what it is allowed to keep","trigger":"learner enters a full name, ID number, or private message into a public AI tool","feedback":"Do not paste identifiers, passwords, or other people's private data into AI tools. Redact or summarize before sharing.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"1d642cd2684d06bc3fc8faa214226b6244c03ce6762de576fe80ae5dd150ba4f","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}